From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:41:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 119/342] kernel: write every page of a new kernel stack once

On arm64 without hardware dirty bit management (the ODROID-C2's
Cortex-A53), a writable page is mapped read-only until its first write,
whose fault sets it writable. That fault can't be taken on the kernel
stack itself: the exception entry pushes its frame into the same page
and faults again. Kernel stacks only got all their pages written by the
debug fill of KDEBUG builds, so a kernel built with KDEBUG_LEVEL 1
panicked at boot: main2 reached the stack's second page, 4.6 KB into it,
inside an exception ("a write to a read-only page of main2's kernel
stack").

create_kernel_stack() now writes one word to every page. Kernel stacks
stay wired, so the pages never become clean again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/kernel/thread.cpp | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/thread.cpp b/src/system/kernel/thread.cpp
index 9bc54cdf35..7bc744d063 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/thread.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/thread.cpp
@@ -235,6 +235,18 @@ create_kernel_stack(void* cookie, void* object)
 
 	addr_t top = base + KERNEL_STACK_SIZE
 		+ KERNEL_STACK_GUARD_PAGES * B_PAGE_SIZE;
+
+	// Write every page once. arm64 without hardware dirty bit management
+	// maps a writable page read-only until its first write faults, and that
+	// fault can't be taken on the kernel stack itself: the exception entry
+	// pushes its frame into the same page and faults again. Kernel stacks
+	// stay wired, so they never become clean again. (Debug builds also fill
+	// the stack on every use, which used to hide this.)
+	for (addr_t page = base + KERNEL_STACK_GUARD_PAGES * B_PAGE_SIZE;
+			page < top; page += B_PAGE_SIZE) {
+		*(volatile uint32*)page = 0;
+	}
+
 	free_kernel_stack(area, base, top);
 	return B_OK;
 }
